In article <1991Jun11.133952.3329@news.cs.brandeis.edu> bis@chaos.cs.brandeis.edu (Bis) writes:

>   Yes, just one: give it a rest.  It drives me crazy to see constant
>   cries of "ABUSE!!  ABUSE!!  ABUSE!!  BURN IT ALIVE, DEV TEAM!  HANG
>   IT!  ABUSE!!" in this newsgroup.  It seems that some people are
>   hellbent on taking all the fun out of Nethack. 

Yes, this statement basically sums up my fears for NH3.1.  It *is*
true that the current version is too easy -- for those in the know --
but for anyone that hasn't read the spoiler file Nethack is an
intensely challenging game. I'm sure somebody must have spent quite a
bit of time thinking up the blessed/cursed bag of holding scam, or the
leprechaun-and- priest scam, in a (largely successful) attempt to make
a very difficult game somewhat less frustrating, only to have their
efforts rewarded by complete obliteration of these features in 3.1 (or
so I gather). Working your way around these (mis-)features is, IMO,
one of the most entertaining aspects of NH.

So what I say is this: leave these features in for all new Hackers to
discover for themselves (and let's face it -- they do take a bit of
ingenuity to work out), but to prevent outright abuse simply limit
their effectiveness to some finite level. Let the bag of holding scam
work, but make the bag explode if its weight gets too negative. Let
the leprechaun steal from the priest, but only for a few times else
the priest gets angry. Let sinks produce black puddings ad nauseam,
but after a dozen or so disgorgings let out something *really* nasty
(and Elbereth- resistant :-). Let the wand of polymorph change your
goodies into monsters if you use it too often. Do anything, but *dont*
take out the best features of Nethack!

Dave.

